      conceptHeading: 'The approach, quickly',
      conceptBody: [
        'In active voice, the subject performs the action ("The cat chased the mouse"). In passive voice, the object of that action becomes the new subject ("The mouse was chased by the cat").',
        'Three moving parts, every time: the object of the active sentence becomes the new subject; the verb becomes "a form of be + past participle"; the original subject moves after "by" (or is dropped if unknown or unimportant).',
        'The tense must be preserved exactly — only the form of "be" changes to match it: simple present → is/are + past participle, simple past → was/were + past participle, present continuous → is/are + being + past participle, and so on.',
        'Only transitive verbs (ones that take a direct object) can become passive. A sentence with no object, like "She slept" or "He arrived," has no passive form at all.',
      ],
