      lede: 'The whole topic comes down to one question: does the order of arrangement matter? Get that right and the correct formula follows automatically. Here’s the concept, four fully worked examples, and a few to try yourself.',
      conceptHeading: 'The concept, quickly',
      conceptBody: [
        'Permutation counts ARRANGEMENTS, where order matters (ABC is different from BCA). Combination counts SELECTIONS, where order doesn’t matter (choosing the group {A, B, C} is the same regardless of order).',
        'Factorial is the building block for both: n! = n × (n−1) × (n−2) × … × 1, and by convention 0! = 1.',
        'Permutation of n items taken r at a time: ⁿPᵣ = n! / (n−r)!. Combination of n items taken r at a time: ⁿCᵣ = n! / (r! × (n−r)!) — a combination is a permutation divided by the r! ways to arrange the chosen r items, since order no longer matters.',
        'Watch the wording: "arrange", "order", "ranks", "different ways to stand in a line" signal permutation. "Choose", "select", "form a committee" (where only the group matters, not who does what within it) signal combination.',
      ],
