      pageTitle: 'Articles (A, An, The) Questions with Solutions',
      lede: '"A"/"an" versus "the" comes down to one question — is this something specific and already identified, or just any one example of its kind? Here’s the approach, four fully worked examples, and a few to try yourself.',
      conceptHeading: 'The approach, quickly',
      conceptBody: [
        '"A" and "an" are indefinite articles, used for one unspecified item from a class, or when introducing something for the first time. "The" is the definite article, used for something already identified, unique, or previously mentioned.',
        'Choose "a" or "an" based on the SOUND of the next word, not its spelling: "an" before a vowel sound (an hour, an MBA), "a" before a consonant sound (a university, a one-rupee coin) — "hour" starts with a silent "h" (a vowel sound), while "university" starts with a "y" consonant sound despite the spelled vowel "u".',
        '"The" is used for something unique in context (the sun, the Prime Minister), something already mentioned ("I saw a dog. The dog was brown."), superlatives (the best, the tallest), and specific well-known geographic features (the Ganges, the Himalayas) — but not before most country/person names, or uncountable nouns used generally.',
        'No article at all is used before plural or uncountable nouns speaking generally ("Dogs are loyal," not "The dogs are loyal," unless referring to specific known dogs), and before most proper nouns.',
      ],
      examplesHeading: 'Worked examples',
