      lede: 'Tag questions run on one flip rule — positive statement, negative tag; negative statement, positive tag — plus matching the auxiliary verb and pronoun exactly. Here’s the approach, four fully worked examples, and a few to try yourself.',
      conceptHeading: 'The approach, quickly',
      conceptBody: [
        'The core rule: if the main statement is POSITIVE, the tag is NEGATIVE; if the main statement is NEGATIVE, the tag is POSITIVE.',
        'The tag always uses the SAME auxiliary verb as the main statement, and a PRONOUN matching the subject — never the subject’s own noun repeated: "Ravi is coming, isn’t he?" not "isn’t Ravi?".',
        'If the main statement has no visible auxiliary (a simple present or simple past sentence with just a main verb), the tag borrows "do/does" (present) or "did" (past): "She likes tea, doesn’t she?"',
        'A few fixed exceptions: "I am" pairs with the tag "aren’t I?" (not "amn’t I"); imperatives ("Close the door") typically take "will you?"; "Let’s" sentences take "shall we?".',
      ],
      examplesHeading: 'Worked examples',
