      lede: 'The same idea can be written as a simple, compound, or complex sentence — the content stays the same, only the grammatical structure changes. Here’s the approach, four fully worked examples, and a few to try yourself.',
      conceptHeading: 'The approach, quickly',
      conceptBody: [
        'A simple sentence has exactly one independent clause (one subject-verb unit) — if the information is split into two separate ideas, it needs to be compressed into one before it can be "simple."',
        'A compound sentence joins two or more independent clauses with a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or, so) or a semicolon — both halves could stand alone as complete sentences.',
        'A complex sentence joins one independent clause with one or more dependent clauses, using a subordinator (because, although, since, when, if, who, which, that) — the dependent clause can’t stand alone.',
        'When converting a complex sentence to simple, the subordinate clause usually compresses into a phrase ("Because it was raining" becomes "Due to the rain") rather than disappearing entirely — the meaning has to survive the transformation.',
      ],
      examplesHeading: 'Worked examples',
